---
title: OIDC sign-in, seen on three layers
---
sequenceDiagram
  box What the user sees
    actor User
    participant Browser as Browser tab
    participant Consent as Login and consent page
  end
  box Protocol and data
    participant BFF as Client backend
    participant AS as Authorization server
    participant API as Resource API
  end
  box Infrastructure
    participant Net as Public internet
    participant ALB as Application load balancer
    participant Sessions as Redis session store
    participant Directory as User directory DB
    participant JWKS as JWKS signing keys
    participant Orders as Postgres orders DB
  end

  %% @zone vpc "VPC": ALB, Sessions, Directory, JWKS, Orders
  %% @zone public in vpc "Public subnet": ALB
  %% @zone private in vpc "Private subnet": Sessions, JWKS
  %% @zone datasub in vpc "Data subnet": Directory, Orders
  %% @kind Net internet

  User->>Browser: Clicks Sign in
  Note over User,Browser: Nothing about OIDC is visible yet. To the user, this is one click.

  Browser->>Net: GET /login over HTTPS
  Note over Browser,Net: The request leaves the device and crosses the public internet.
  Net->>ALB: Reaches the public subnet
  Note over Net,ALB: The load balancer terminates TLS. Only it is exposed to the internet.
  ALB->>BFF: Forwards to client backend
  Note over ALB,BFF: Back up a layer: infrastructure has delivered the request to the app.

  BFF->>Sessions: Stores state, nonce, PKCE verifier
  Note over BFF,Sessions: These secrets stay server-side. They later prove the response belongs to this login.
  BFF-->>Browser: 302 to /authorize with code challenge
  Note over BFF,Browser: The redirect carries only the hash of the PKCE verifier, never the verifier.

  Browser->>Consent: Opens the login page
  Note over Browser,Consent: The user is now on the identity provider's domain. The client app never sees the password.
  User->>Consent: Types password and MFA code
  Note over User,Consent: Credentials go only to the identity provider.
  Consent->>AS: Submits credentials
  Note over Consent,AS: The hosted page posts to the authorization server.
  AS->>Directory: Verifies password hash
  Note over AS,Directory: Down to the data subnet. Only the hash is compared.
  Directory-->>AS: Returns user record
  Note over Directory,AS: The record supplies the subject and claims for the ID token.

  AS-->>Browser: 302 to /callback with code and state
  Note over AS,Browser: The authorization code is short-lived and useless without the PKCE verifier.
  Browser->>BFF: GET /callback with code and state
  Note over Browser,BFF: Still only a redirect from the user's point of view.

  BFF->>Sessions: Loads state and verifier
  Note over BFF,Sessions: The state must match, which blocks login CSRF.
  Sessions-->>BFF: Returns PKCE verifier
  Note over Sessions,BFF: The verifier comes back for the token exchange.
  BFF->>AS: POST /token with code and verifier
  Note over BFF,AS: This back-channel call never touches the browser.
  AS->>JWKS: Signs tokens with private key
  Note over AS,JWKS: The signing key stays in the private subnet.
  AS-->>BFF: Returns ID token and access token
  Note over AS,BFF: The ID token says who signed in. The access token says what the app may do.

  BFF->>Sessions: Saves tokens against session id
  Note over BFF,Sessions: Tokens stay on the server. This is why the pattern is called a backend for frontend.
  BFF-->>Browser: Sets HttpOnly session cookie
  Note over BFF,Browser: The browser only gets an opaque cookie that scripts cannot read.
  Browser->>User: Shows signed-in dashboard
  Note over Browser,User: The user is in. Everything after this is an ordinary API call.

  Browser->>BFF: GET /orders with cookie
  Note over Browser,BFF: The cookie identifies the session, and the backend swaps it for the access token.
  BFF->>API: GET /orders with bearer token
  Note over BFF,API: The API trusts the token, not the caller.
  API->>JWKS: Fetches public key
  Note over API,JWKS: The API verifies the signature locally, so it can cache this key.
  API->>Orders: SELECT orders for subject
  Note over API,Orders: The token's subject claim limits which rows come back.
  Orders-->>API: Returns rows
  Note over Orders,API: Authorization happened before the query ran.
  API-xBrowser: 403 forbidden for admin report
  Note over API,Browser: A token without the right scope is refused. Authentication is not authorization.
